## 3. OAuth2 login method mapping

`ServerHttpSecurity.OAuth2LoginSpec` has reactive equivalents like `authenticationSuccessHandler`, `authenticationFailureHandler`, `authenticationMatcher`, `authorizationRequestRepository`, `authorizationRequestResolver`, `authorizedClientRepository`, and `clientRegistrationRepository`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/api/java/org/springframework/security/config/web/server/ServerHttpSecurity.OAuth2LoginSpec.html))

| WebMVC `oauth2Login()` | WebFlux `oauth2Login()` |
|---|---|
| `redirectEndpoint().baseUri("/login/oauth2/callback/*")` | `.authenticationMatcher(new PathPatternParserServerWebExchangeMatcher("/login/oauth2/callback/{registrationId}"))` |
| `defaultSuccessUrl("/home")` | `.authenticationSuccessHandler(new RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler("/home"))` |
| `defaultSuccessUrl("/home", true)` | `RedirectServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler("/home")` + `setRequestCache(NoOpServerRequestCache.getInstance())` |
| `successHandler(...)` | `.authenticationSuccessHandler(ServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler)` |
| `failureHandler(...)` | `.authenticationFailureHandler(ServerAuthenticationFailureHandler)` |
| `tokenEndpoint()` | Usually replace with a `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest>` bean or a custom reactive `authenticationManager` |

For a custom redirect/callback path, WebFlux’s default authorization response endpoint is `/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`. If you change it with `authenticationMatcher`, also update the `ClientRegistration.redirectUri`, for example: `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/callback/{registrationId}`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/7.0/reactive/oauth2/login/advanced.html))

---

## 4. Token endpoint / authorization code grant conversion

In WebMVC you may have something like:

```java
.oauth2Login(oauth2 -> oauth2
    .tokenEndpoint(token -> token
        .accessTokenResponseClient(myClient)
    )
)
```

or:

```java
.oauth2Client(oauth2 -> oauth2
    .authorizationCodeGrant(code -> code
        .accessTokenResponseClient(myClient)
    )
)
```

In WebFlux, the preferred approach is usually to publish a reactive token response client bean:

```java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.core.convert.converter.Converter;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.endpoint.OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.endpoint.ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient;
import org.springframework.security.oauth2.client.endpoint.WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient;
import org.springframework.util.LinkedMultiValueMap;
import org.springframework.util.MultiValueMap;

@Bean
ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest>
authorizationCodeAccessTokenResponseClient() {
    WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient client =
            new WebClientReactiveAuthorizationCodeTokenResponseClient();

    client.addParametersConverter(extraTokenParameters());

    return client;
}

private Converter<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest, MultiValueMap<String, String>>
extraTokenParameters() {
    return grantRequest -> {
        MultiValueMap<String, String> parameters = new LinkedMultiValueMap<>();
        parameters.set("audience", "your-api");
        return parameters;
    };
}
```

Spring Security’s WebFlux OAuth2 docs show that publishing a `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient<OAuth2AuthorizationCodeGrantRequest>` bean customizes the `authorization_code` token request, and it is picked up by Spring Security’s OAuth2 client components. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/7.0/reactive/oauth2/index.html))

