## 5. Logout conversion

| WebMVC `logout()` | WebFlux `logout()` |
|---|---|
| `invalidateHttpSession(true)` | Add `new WebSessionServerLogoutHandler()` |
| `clearAuthentication(true)` | `SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler` |
| `deleteCookies("X")` | Custom `ServerLogoutHandler` that adds expired `ResponseCookie`, or use `Clear-Site-Data` header |
| `logoutUrl("/logout")` | `.logoutUrl("/logout")` |
| `addLogoutHandler(handler)` | In WebFlux, use `logoutHandler(...)`; if using multiple handlers, use `DelegatingServerLogoutHandler` |

WebFlux logout has a default logout endpoint, and session invalidation is done by adding `WebSessionServerLogoutHandler`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/7.0/reactive/authentication/logout.html?utm_source=openai)) `ServerHttpSecurity.LogoutSpec` exposes `logoutHandler`, `logoutUrl`, `requiresLogout`, and `logoutSuccessHandler`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/7.0-SNAPSHOT/api/java/org/springframework/security/config/web/server/ServerHttpSecurity.LogoutSpec.html))

---

## 6. Session management conversion

This is where WebFlux differs the most.

| WebMVC `sessionManagement()` | WebFlux equivalent |
|---|---|
| `sessionCreationPolicy(...)` | No direct WebFlux equivalent |
| `sessionFixation(...)` | Usually no explicit config; `WebSessionServerSecurityContextRepository` changes the session id when saving the `SecurityContext` |
| `invalidSessionStrategy(...)` | No direct DSL equivalent; usually handle expired/missing session in exception handling or application logic |
| `maximumSessions(...)` / concurrency | `.sessionManagement(s -> s.concurrentSessions(...))` |
| `SessionRegistry` | `ReactiveSessionRegistry` |

The WebFlux `SessionManagementSpec` is focused on concurrent sessions; its documented reactive DSL exposes `concurrentSessions(...)`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/site/docs/current/api/org/springframework/security/config/web/server/ServerHttpSecurity.SessionManagementSpec.html)) For concurrent session control, Spring Security can monitor Form Login, OAuth2 Login, and HTTP Basic logins, and you configure limits with `maximumSessions(...)`. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/7.0/reactive/authentication/concurrent-sessions-control.html))

Example:

```java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.security.core.session.ReactiveSessionRegistry;
import org.springframework.security.core.session.InMemoryReactiveSessionRegistry;
import org.springframework.security.web.server.authentication.SessionLimit;

@Bean
SecurityWebFilterChain springSecurityFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http) {
    return http
            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges
                    .anyExchange().authenticated()
            )
            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())
            .sessionManagement(session -> session
                    .concurrentSessions(concurrency -> concurrency
                            .maximumSessions(SessionLimit.of(1))
                    )
            )
            .build();
}

@Bean
ReactiveSessionRegistry reactiveSessionRegistry() {
    return new InMemoryReactiveSessionRegistry();
}
```

If your Spring Security version uses the older API names, the equivalent may be `maxSessions(1)` / `maxSessionsPreventsLogin(true)` instead of `maximumSessions(SessionLimit.of(1))`.

---

## 7. Stateless note

If you used this in WebMVC:

```java
.sessionManagement(session ->
    session.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)
)
```

there is no exact WebFlux equivalent. For a **stateless API**, you normally avoid `oauth2Login()` and use `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` instead. If you still want to prevent saving the `SecurityContext`, use:

```java
import org.springframework.security.web.server.context.NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository;

http.securityContextRepository(NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.getInstance());
```

`NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository` is the reactive no-op security context repository used for stateless applications. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/site/docs/5.8.0-M1/api/org/springframework/security/web/server/context/NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.html?utm_source=openai)) Be careful: browser-based `oauth2Login()` generally needs session state for the authorization request, CSRF/state correlation, and login persistence.

---

## Short answer

Use this mental conversion:

```java
HttpSecurity
```

becomes:

```java
ServerHttpSecurity
```

and servlet handlers like:

```java
AuthenticationSuccessHandler
AuthenticationFailureHandler
LogoutHandler
LogoutSuccessHandler
```

become:

```java
ServerAuthenticationSuccessHandler
ServerAuthenticationFailureHandler
ServerLogoutHandler
ServerLogoutSuccessHandler
```

Also, `redirectEndpoint().baseUri(...)` becomes `authenticationMatcher(...)`, `tokenEndpoint()` customization usually becomes a `ReactiveOAuth2AccessTokenResponseClient` bean, and session/logout behavior must be rewritten around `WebSession` and `ServerLogoutHandler`.
